\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Rupiah at 7-year low, won at 3-wk trough\b0\par\par\uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2008\par 08:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI, Nov 18 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> hit a fresh 7-year low against the dollar on Tuesday, once again at the fore of declines in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  spurred by falling stock markets and increasing evidence pointing to a global recession.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah fell 2.9 percent to 12,050 to the U.S. dollar, with some dealers expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken towards 12,100 in the coming week due to market worries over foreign fund outflows from the country's financial market.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=> fell to a three-week closing low, while the Taiwan dollar <TWD=> declined to a two-week intraday trough as more key economies, such as Japan and the euro zone, slipped into a recession.\par \par \uc2 "It's mainly the bearish global market hitting the rupiah. Stocks all around are falling and market sentiment is quite weak," a dealer in Indonesia said.\par \par \uc2 "Indonesia's GDP figures pressured the rupiah, but the overall impact was limited," the dealer said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to defend its falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has fallen 21 percent this year. On Tuesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it will not let the rupiah hit irrational levels.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Indonesia, southeast Asia's biggest economy, reported third quarter growth of 6.1 percent, easing from the second quarter, mainly because exports slowed.\par \par \uc2 "We think financial turmoil associated with the rupiah's weakness will slow fourth quarter growth," ING said in a report.\par \par \uc2 Economies in emerging Asia, such as South Korea and Taiwan, are largely powered by exports and a global downturn will likely sap demand for the region's produce, such as electronics gadgets, cars and textiles.\par \par \uc2 Japan's recession could be longer than previously expected and governments around the world are doing everything to try and boost domestic consumption, with Taiwan offering to dole out shopping vouchers to its citizens.\par \par \uc2 The Korean won <KRW=> ended 2.7 percent lower at 1,447.9/9.1 per dollar, the Taiwan dollar hit a three-week intraday low of 33.290, while the Singapore dollar lost 0.2 percent to trade at 1.5261, hovering at an over one-year low. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par   Change on the day at 0637 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        96.55          96.35       -0.21\par  Sing dlr        1.5261         1.5228       -0.22\par  Taiwan dlr      33.255         33.170       -0.26\par  Korean won     1447.90        1409.00       -2.69\par  Baht             35.00          34.98       -0.06\par  Peso             49.73          49.73       +0.00\par  Rupiah        11850.00       11700.00       -1.27\par  Rupee            49.72          49.34       -0.75\par  Ringgit         3.5985         3.5980       -0.01\par  Yuan            6.8263         6.8269       +0.01\par  Change so far in 2008\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        96.55         111.33      +15.31\par  Sing dlr        1.5261         1.4382       -5.76\par  Taiwan dlr      33.255         32.443       -2.44\par  Korean won     1447.90         935.70      -35.38\par  Baht             35.00          33.68       