\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asians plumb multi-year lows on growth fears\b0\par\par\uc2 542 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2008\par 04:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 23 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah hit its lowest level in nearly three years as Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to new multi-year lows on Thursday, hit by investor fears over global growth and persistent selling of risky assets.\par \par \uc2  A day after Hungary raised rates by a hefty 3 percentage points to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , risk aversion was elevated in Asia with stock markets in Korea, Indonesia and the Philippines falling between 4 and 7 percent.\par \par \uc2  The rupiah <IDR=> hit its lowest in nearly 3 years at 9,950 a dollar, the Philippine peso weakened to its weakest since early 2007 at 49 per dollar while the Malaysian ringgit fell to 3.5771, a nearly 2-year trough.\par \par \uc2  The broad selling in emerging markets seemed to have sucked Asia in deep, even though it is backed by better fundamentals, such as ample reserves and growth estimates in higher single digits even in dismal global circumstances.\par \par \uc2  "The nasty part is that deleveraging/balance sheet hole-plugging can produce indiscriminate selling, so Asia can be swept up even though its fundamentals are better," said Sean Callow, a strategist at Westpac Bank\par \par \uc2  Central banks in India, \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Korea and the Philippines have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' decline and some of them were suspected of being in the market on Thursday.\par \par \uc2  Analysts reckon policy makers are however merely smoothing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation, and are tolerant of the weakness at a time when the risks of recession in Asia's main export markets are as high as that of heavy capital outflows triggered by the credit crisis.\par \par \uc2  "Adding liquidity to increasingly thin and volatile markets is a valid policy decision, and note that \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of most economies are ample," Patrick Bennett a strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2  "We would only become concerned with a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  attempting to defend a particular level, or one which was clearly acting contrary to economic fundamentals."  \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0526 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        97.43          97.82       +0.40\par  Sing dlr        1.5006         1.4991       -0.10\par  Taiwan dlr      33.367         32.990       -1.13\par  Korean won     1407.90        1363.00       -3.19\par  Peso             48.96          48.74       -0.45\par  Rupiah         9950.00        9895.00       -0.55\par  Rupee            49.74          49.30       -0.88\par  Ringgit         3.5670         3.5440       -0.64\par  Yuan            6.8369         6.8341       -0.04\par  Change so far in 2008\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        97.43         111.33      +14.27\par  Sing dlr        1.5006         1.4382       -4.16\par  Taiwan dlr      33.367         32.443       -2.77\par  Korean won     1407.90         935.70      -33.54\par  Baht             34.49          33.68       -2.35\par  Peso             48.96          41.28      -15.69\par  Rupiah         9950.00        9390.00       -5.63\par  Rupee            49.74          39.41      -20.77\par  Ringgit         3.5670         3.3050       -7.35