\par\par \b \uc2 4th UPDATE: Asian Shares Drop On US Econ Woes; Shippers Sink\b0\par\par\uc2 1,392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2008\par 09:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates levels)\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian share markets skidded Thursday with financial and shipping stocks under water as concerns mounted about the global economy amid signs consumer spending was flagging heading into the end-year holiday season.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei 225 led the way after large gains earlier this week, ending down 11.4% for its largest one-day percentage drop since October 1987.\par \par \uc2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 shed 6.8% with Korea's Kospi Composite ending down 9.4% in its largest one-day percentage fall since September 2001.\par \par \uc2 "A day is a long time in financial markets, let alone a week. The unwinding of most of the early-week gains on many bourses suggests there is still a large segment of the market standing ready to sell into bounces," said the analysis team at Westpac Institutional Bank.\par \par \uc2 "Certainly no-one can have confidence that we are near the end of the wave of mutual fund and hedge fund redemptions."\par \par \uc2 European spreadbetting firms were tipping stocks there to open lower with BGC Partners forecasting London's FTSE-100 down 214 points.\par \par \uc2 But in a slightly unusual dislocation, riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Australian dollar made a cautious recovery against the U.S. dollar and the Japanese yen; some said that was a sign of the return of the market focus to the negatives stacking up against the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 "What is hammering home is that people are getting beyond the financial crisis and seeing implications for the broader economy. Growth is looking pretty dismal," said Bank of New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Danica Hampton in Wellington.\par \par \uc2 Reflecting those concerns, base metal and crude oil prices dropped in Asia, with LME copper down 5%. Overnight, mining giant \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  said it would reassess expansion spending plans and curtail production of aluminum.\par \par \uc2 Shipping stocks around Asia fell on a slump in the Baltic Dry Index, a benchmark for rates on the bulk shipping sector; the index sank 10.7% to 1615 Wednesday in what freight derivatives broker FIS called a "day of disbelief," and was down more than 86% from its peak in May.\par \par \uc2 "I feel like we are in a deep sea with nothing we can do for now," said Taurus Investment & Securities analyst Oh Tae-Dong.\par \par \uc2 Traders were on the lookout for redemption selling, with talk of mutual funds offloading shares in the U.S. overnight.\par \par \uc2 But, for now, volume was on the light side. "We haven't seen panic selling. The mood is very, very cautious," said First NZ Capital broker James Snell.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures were down 1.5%-2% in screen trade; \uc2 Ebay Inc\uc2 . fell 5.6% after-hours as it cut its fourth-quarter revenue outlook and earnings projections to below what analysts were expecting; also, its Chief Executive John Donahoe said the company expected a "very challenging" fourth quarter holiday season.\par \par \uc2 Earnings reports are due out later in the U.S. from such key names as \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  and \uc2 Advanced Micro Devices\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The moves in Asia followed the S&P 500's biggest percentage loss since Oct. 19, 1987; the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 7.9% after September U.S. retail sales fell the most in three years.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei 225 was hurt by declines in financial stocks, plus commodity-linked plays and those with exposure to consumer sentiment and/or a stronger yen; \uc2 Sony\uc2  fell 12.9% with \uc2 Inpex Holdings\uc2  down 14.2%, while \uc2 Elpida Memory\uc2  dived 16.9% to a limit-low and year-low of Y983.\par \par \uc2 The Topix index of all the Tokyo Stock Exchange First Section issues fell 90.99 points, or 9.5%, to 864.52, while December Nikkei 225 futures ended down 1240 points, or 13%, at 8250 on the Osaka Securities Exchange.\par \par \uc2 In Australia the share index ended near its lowest level since May 2005, as commodity price weakness hurt mining stocks; BHP Billiton fell 13.1% and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  15.9% while Woodside shed 5.5%.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand's market ended down 4.8% at its lowest level since September 2004; bellwether Telecom sank 8.6% to its lowest level since its 1991 listing, after cutting its profit forecasts.\par \par \uc2 Korea suffered its biggest point fall in the index's history as foreigners dumped shares worth KRW635.4 billion; KB Financial Group fell the daily limit of 15% with \uc2 Shinhan Financial Group\uc2  down 11.2%.\par \par \uc2 Steelmaker \uc2 Posco\uc2  also fell its 15% daily limit while the world's largest shipbuilder by orders, \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2 , was down the 15% limit, too, as were \uc2 Hanjin Shipping\uc2  and Korea Line.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shipping stocks were hurting the market there with the Hang Seng Index down 8.4%; China Shipping Development was down 25.1% at HK$5.89, China Cosco off 16.1% and Pacific Basin down 6.1%.\par \par \uc2 India's Sensex was 7% lower after briefly touching a 2008 trough; there wasn't much support from late Wednesday's news of another cash requirement ratio cut from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Taiwan shares ended down 3.3% with volume slowing to a four-year low.\par \par \uc2 Philippine shares finished with a fall 5.2% with Singapore's Straits Times Index recently off 7.3% and the Shanghai Composite Index down 3%; Thai shares were lower by 4.7% and Indonesia 4.6%.\par \par \uc2 Among \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the U.S. dollar was around Y100.17 after falling to Y99.28 and the euro at Y134.12 after touching Y133.39, though against the dollar it had fallen to $1.3380.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar was back near US$0.6675, from an early fall to US$0.6521, and was showing some resilience against the yen too, as was the New Zealand dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Investor fears are increasingly focusing on threats to the real economy and in the wake of the soft U.S. retail data released overnight and some further easing of dollar funding pressures, the U.S. dollar is losing some of its recent shine," said \uc2 Societe Generale\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Patrick Bennett.\par \par \uc2 But Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained weak: The U.S. dollar rose sharply against the Korean won and hit an 18-month high against the Philippine peso; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sell dollars was suspected in India, \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Philippines.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasurys were boosted by a flight to safety with the 10-year note up 17/32 at 100 15/32, yielding 3.940% and the two-year up 5/32 at 100 27/32, to yield 1.551%.\par \par \uc2 Lead 10-year Japanese government bond futures were helped by a solid auction of five-year notes with the contract at 134.91, up 0.11.\par \par \uc2 Credit default spreads blew wider with the Markit iTraxx Asia ex-Japan high-yield index quoted at 890-930 basis points, from 790-820 basis points Wednesday; the investment-grade index was 320-325 basis points, versus 260-265.\par \par \uc2 LME 3-month copper was down 4% at $4,700 a metric ton with notable falls too in zinc and nickel.\par \par \uc2 "It's not wise in the current environment to stick one's head out. I expect more downward pressure on metals prices across the board," said \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  analyst Yingxi Yu; "the whole complex will be under pressure as long as the financial situation doesn't get better and there's more confidence in the economy."\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was lower, down $6.10 at $842 a troy ounce, hurt by the commodity-wide pullback which also curbed silver and platinum.\par \par \uc2 Front-month Nymex crude was down $2.14 on Globex at $72.40 per barrel, after falling $4.09 in New York; it briefly touched its lowest level since last August, at $72.31.\par \par \uc2 The fall in oil was reducing gold's appeal as an inflation hedge.\par \par \uc2 -By Rosalind Mathieson, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4140; Rosalind.Mathieson@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 16-10-08 0700GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081016e4ag000ao\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
