\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia slides as stocks pounded, won trades wildly\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao   \par \uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2008\par 08:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 10 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were battered on Friday as investors dumped stocks amid the financial turmoil despite a round of global interest rate cuts, prompting dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in South Korea, \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and India.\par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> fell as far as 9,750 per dollar, down about 1.6 percent from Thursday's close, as trader cited dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "The action keeps players sidelined," said a trader in Jakarta.\par \par \uc2  The South Korean won <KRW=> moved wildly, gaining over 12 percent to 1,224.9 per dollar at one point after falling sharply earlier, as the authorities were seen selling dollars to defend the won, which has lost 27 percent against the dollar this year.\par \par \uc2  A wave of selling pounded Asian shares amid growing fears of the financial turmoil, with the MSCI index of Asia-Pacific stocks excluding Japan <.MIAPJ0000PUS> falling 7.7 percent to its lowest since January 2005.\par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee <INR=IN> hit a record low at 49.25 per dollar, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would cut bank's cash reserve requirements to 7.5 percent to ease a liquidity crunch. [ID:nBOM357479]\par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Singapore dollar <SGD=> fell to 1.4865 per U.S dollar, giving up its early gains as investors turned bearish on data showing the economy is in recession.\par \par \uc2  "It's due to the GDP figure," said a trader in Singapore.\par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar briefly rose to 1.4724 immediately after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced an easing in monetary policy.\par \par \uc2  Some traders said suspected official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the local unit.\par \par \uc2  Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which sets policy by managing the Singapore dollar in a secret trade-weighted band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , eased monetary policy for the first time in five years after its economy sank into recession [ID:nSIN389047]\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday it was shifting policy from a gradual appreciation bias to a neutral zero-appreciation bias for the trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in response to slowing growth and expected easing in inflation.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintained the width of the secretive band.\par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso <PHP=> lost almost 0.9 percent to 47.8 per dollar, while the Thai baht <THB=TH> was down 0.4 percent to 34.48 per dollar.\par \par \uc2  "We see some interest selling from exporters and some from offshore," said a trader in Bangkok. "The bias is that the dollar should go up against the baht, like dollar/regionals."\par \par \uc2  Markets in Taiwan are closed for a holiday.\par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the cost of dollar funding remained elevated in Asia on Friday, with overnight dollar funds <USDOND=> quoted between 4 and 7 percent in Singapore, 4.5-5.8 percent in Bangkok and 4-5 percent in Jakarta, traders said.\par \par \uc2  The levels held largely steady from Thursday, when several Asian central banks joined a worldwide rate-cutting campaign to contain the global financial crisis that has forced commercial banks to hoard cash, rather than lend to each other.  \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0556 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        99.30          99.52       +0.22\par  Sing dlr        1.4800         1.4737       -0.43\par  Korean won     1269.90        1379.50       +8.63\par  Baht             34.39          34.33       -0.17\par  Peso             47.80          47.39       -0.86\par  Rupiah         9750.00        9590.00       -1.64\par  Rupee            48.71          48.00       -1.46\par  Ringgit         3.5100         3.5055       -0.13\par  Yuan            6.8331         6.8205       -0.18\par  Change so far in 2008\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        99.30         111.33      +12.11\par  Sing dlr        1.4800         1.4382       -2.82\par  Taiwan dlr      32.438         32.443       +0.02\par  Korean won     1269.90         935.70      -26.32\par  Baht             34.39          33.68       -2.06\par  Peso             47.80          41.28      -13.64\par  Rupiah         9750.00        9390.00       -3.69\par  Rupee            48.71          39.41      -19.09\par  Ringgit         3.5100         3.3050       -5.84\par  Yuan            6.8331         7.3041       +6.89\par  For the new Reuters scrolling global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  service please\par click <FXNEWS>\par   (Editing by Jacqueline Wong)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|IF|PHD|SI|Z|J|SNS|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081013e4aa000ee\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
