\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Asian governments act to prop up stocks,  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 793 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2008\par 12:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Asian governments step in, tighten stock selling rules\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  prohibits short selling in October\par \par \uc2 * Thai, Philippine central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Rafael Nam\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Sept 30 (Reuters) - Asian governments from South Korea to Indonesia stepped in swiftly on Tuesday to try to prop up markets reeling from an overnight plunge in the United States after U.S. lawmakers rejected a $700 billion plan rescue plan for the financial industry.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the region, finance authorities and central banks spoke up to defend the soundness of their economies and acted by tightening stock-selling rules and buying tumbling \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hurt by the flight of money from emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 In Taiwan, the president, vice premier, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and financial regulator made moves and comments to ease jittery markets.\par \par \uc2 "Taiwan's economic fundamentals are still good," President Ma Ying-jeou said in a speech at a local event. "We have the means to weather this global financial crisis."\par \par \uc2 India's finance minister, Palaniappan Chidambaram, stepped in to assure investors that market regulations would be tightened if needed.\par \par \uc2 "There is nothing to worry about. The regulations that are in place are adequate. If the regulations are to be tweaked, we will do so," he told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Malaysian Deputy Prime Minister Najib Razak said the government was maintaining its growth forecast of 5.5-5.8 percent for the year, adding the country was on track with opening its markets despite the Wall Street collapse.\par \par \uc2 Among Asia's financial market watchdogs, Taiwan's top financial regulator placed tighter limits on the short-selling of stocks, while Hong Kong's Securities and Futures Commission warned it would not tolerate abusive practices and could impose market-wide controls.\par \par \uc2 OUTRIGHT BANS\par \par \uc2 Other governments moved to outright bans.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's stock exchange said it would prohibit short selling of stocks in October, and South Korea said it would do so for the time being while at the same time increasing the amount of shares firms are allowed to buy back.\par \par \uc2 The restrictions or outright bans on short selling follow similar actions by U.S. and European regulators this month, and come as Asian stocks head in September for their biggest monthly slide since the financial crisis a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 The failure on Monday of Washington's biggest and most comprehensive bid to keep financial sector shockwaves from tearing up the real economy handed the U.S. S&P 500 stock index its biggest decline since October 1987 and the Dow Jones industrial average its biggest points fall on record.\par \par \uc2 Asian stocks suffered heavily in September, victims of a sell-first-and-ask-later mentality among global investors. With risk aversion widespread, emerging market holdings have been among the first to be sold, analysts have said.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI index if Asia-Pacific stocks outside Japan is down about 16 percent in September, similar to the slide seen in the same month eight years ago and potentially its largest decline since October 1997.\par \par \uc2 "Despite the fact that the U.S. is at the centre of the crisis, every time the crisis escalates, capital leaves emerging Asia," said Citigroup analysts in a note to clients on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "This is probably a result of flight to quality. But it also provides strong evidence (that) refute the so-called decoupling thesis," they added.\par \par \uc2 The tighter rules on short-selling, combined with expectations that U.S. lawmakers will eventually approve a rescue plan helped many Asian stock markets pull back from their day's lows, but analysts expect broad risk reduction to continue as investors pull money out of the region.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  UNDER FIRE\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have also come under fire this month.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean won dropped 1.5 percent on Tuesday, bringing its loss for September to 9.8 percent, its biggest monthly percentage loss since December 1997. Its loss for the year so far is well over 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 The falls on Tuesday came despite some talk over dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities and even after the Finance Minister said the country would spend part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to stabilise markets if necessary.\par \par \uc2 South Korean authorities have spent more than $30 billion this year to try to underpin the won.\par \par \uc2 Other governments also moved to support their ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars on Tuesday to prop up the peso, while the Bank of Thailand \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the baht to keep its moves in line with other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  though it did not specify which action had been taken. (Writing by Valerie Lee; Editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/EMERGING (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080930e49u000rk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pic