\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: PBOC Cuts Rates; Policy Shift To Aid China Econ\b0\par\par\uc2 1,023 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2008\par 14:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds analysis, details of actions by other central banks.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Terence Poon and Victoria Ruan \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rates for the first time in six years Monday, abruptly reversing course and signaling Beijing may step in with more measures to support an economy hurt by the global slowdown.\par \par \uc2 The People's \uc2 Bank of China\uc2 's announced the rate cut just hours after turmoil engulfed the U.S. financial sector, with \uc2 Lehman Brothers Holdings Inc\uc2 . seeking bankruptcy protection from creditors and \uc2 Merrill Lynch & Co\uc2 . agreeing to be rescued by \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The PBOC's policy easing caught many analysts off guard, though most said it likely wasn't directly driven by the dramatic news on Wall Street. China's "government is more concerned than we thought about the prospects of growth," said Standard Chartered economist Stephen Green.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its benchmark lending rate effective Tuesday and reduced the portion of reserves that smaller financial companies must set aside, particularly those in areas that were hit hard by a devastating earthquake in May.\par \par \uc2 As China's inflation has steadily slowed, the PBOC had recently been hinting that it would fine-tune its two-year-old tightening policy. But few expected such a dramatic easing so suddenly.\par \par \uc2 Monday's measures are intended to "solve the prominent economic problems" and to "maintain and sustain steady growth of the economy," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on its Web site. The PBOC said the moves are in line with second-half economic tasks set by top policymakers at a key meeting in late July.\par \par \uc2 The cuts in lending rates and the reserve ratio are the latest moves by Beijing to support growth, ending a round of policy tightening that started in April 2006. Beijing had previously been focused on taming inflation and curbing economic overheating, but consumer-price rises have been moderating and officials have increasingly turned their focus to sustaining economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The PBOC cut the one-year yuan lending rate by 27 basis points to 7.20%, the first cut since February 2002, and cut the required reserve ratio for smaller banks by 100 basis points from Sept. 25, the first cut since mid-2006.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect Chinese shares to rally when they reopen from a three-day holiday weekend Tuesday, but any gains could be hard to sustain amid persistent concerns about the health of the domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move will likely prompt the index to open higher and then head south again because I think investors already have lots of bad news to digest," said Xu Yinhui, analyst at Guotai Junan Securities.\par \par \uc2 Bond yields and money market interest rates are expected to fall, though one Shanghai-based dealer at a foreign bank predicted the yuan could rise slightly.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said more pro-growth policies, such as lifting the ceiling on bank lending and introducing fresh fiscal stimulus, are likely. Standard Chartered's Green said Beijing is "just around the corner" from ending its ceiling on bank lending and the chances of fiscal policy stimulus "just went up a lot."\par \par \uc2 Since the late-July meeting, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lifted this year's lending quota, widely seen as Beijing's most effective tool for slowing credit and investment growth. China has also since raised the export-tax rebate on certain textile products.\par \par \uc2 The PBOC Monday left the one-year deposit rate unchanged. This could help maintain consumption at a time when China's exporters are hurting from a slowdown in advanced economies that could get worse as Wall Street's troubles mount.\par \par \uc2 Before the latest announcement, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had raised the benchmark one-year lending rate eight times since April 2006, and the one-year deposit rate seven times since August 2006. China had raised the reserve requirement ratio to 17.5% for most commercial banks from 14.5% at the start of the year. The government has been raising the reserve ratio since mid-2006 to drain liquidity as its huge \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves continue to grow.\par \par \uc2 Other central banks in Asia could come under increased pressure to follow China's lead and cut the cost of borrowing as the U.S. economy, a key destination for Asia's exports, shows more signs of trouble.\par \par \uc2 While many central bankers have been focused on fighting inflation with tighter policy, worries about growth are coming increasingly to the fore and some could conclude that rates have gone as high as they need to.\par \par \uc2 Although many of Asia's biggest markets were closed Monday for a holiday, central bankers and regulators across the region were closely watching the unfolding events on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 The PBOC "might have long been prepared" for Monday's moves, but Lehman's troubles "might have encouraged them to move faster," said \uc2 China International Capital Corp\uc2 . analyst Xu Jian.\par \par \uc2 Financial authorities elsewhere in the region also took action Monday.\par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of Australia injected A$1.3 billion into the financial system to ensure ongoing access to cash while South Korea's Financial Services Commission said the authorities would provide foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity, as necessary.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Monday, selling dollars to prop up the rupee, traders said. Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  where necessary and "stands ready" to stabilize the Jakarta's markets. It said Monday it would cut the amount of bonds it issues this year as part of that.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Thailand Deputy Gov. Bandid Nijathaworn warned that the fallout in the U.S. banking sector would prompt investors to move funds out of Asia to inject liquidity into U.S. assets.\par \par \uc2 "We'll have to closely monitor the capital-outflow situation. The (outflow) trend has been continuing for sometime," Bandid told reporters. "The capital movement will affect liquidity in the domestic financial market."\par \par \uc2 -By Terence Poon and Victoria Ruan; Dow Jones Newswires; 8610 6588-5848; victoria.ruan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -Denis McMahon also contributed to this story. [ 15-09-08 1252GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080915e49f000mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
