\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Indonesia c.bank sees cautious response to  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adriana Nina Kusuma and Telly Nathalia   \par \uc2 464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2008\par 07:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts with c.bank gov remarks, adds details, quotes)\par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Sept 12 (Reuters) - Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said on Friday the authority would continue to be in the market to ensure stability for the rupiah <IDR=> but policy responses would be cautious. The rupiah fell on Friday to a seven-month low and has weakened since earlier August -- a trend that has accelerated in the past week -- raising speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to keep monetary policy tighter for longer.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah fell on Friday to 9,465 per dollar, its lowest level since January.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been hit in recent days as investors worried about a withdrawal of foreign money from the high-yielding bond market after seeing the deterioration in the country's trade account.\par \par \uc2 Global risk aversion has also flared again this week, which often means that emerging markets take a hit as investors pare back their riskier holdings.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah recorded its biggest one-day fall in 15 months on Sept. 5, which dealers said had prompted Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BI) to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We will always be in the market," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Boediono, replied when asked about the rupiah's weakness.\par \par \uc2 He has said in the past the phrase does not necessarily mean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It could also refer to market monitoring, he has said.\par \par \uc2 "We certainly have to respond to these changes in a fundamental way, meaning we will respond to changes which are fundamental, not changes that are temporary," Boediono told reporters.\par \par \uc2 "I think our response should not be excessive, whether in regards to interest rates or exchange rates."\par \par \uc2 However, Vice-president Jusuf Kalla said the fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not be cause for concern. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's level was still in line with budget projections and a weaker rupiah would support exports.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's major exports include palm oil, cocoa and rubber.\par \par \uc2 "Of course, for importers and consumers (the rupiah fall) is not good, but it is good for exporters," the former businessman from the country's commodities producing island of Sulawesi told reporters.\par \par \uc2 "The state budget targets (the rupiah) at around the current levels, between 9,400-9,500 (per dollar). So we do not need to worry with (the rupiah) at the current levels."\par \par \uc2 The stock market has also fallen sharply. The Indonesia Composite Index <.JJSE> has fallen more than 11 percent this week, its deepest weekly fall since August 2007. LINKS > For Boediono and Kalla comments.[ID:nJAK83737] (Additional reporting by Tyagita Silka and Andreas Ismar) (Writing by Harry Suhartono, editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 INDONESIA-RUPIAH/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|IF|ABX|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080912e49c001rx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
