\par\par \b \uc2 SE Asian Stocks-S'pore off 2-yr lows, Jakarta down on  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  fears\b0\par\par\uc2 518 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2008\par 12:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  *Singapore bounces off two-year lows as property stocks rise\par \par \uc2  *Jakarta falls to 18-mth low on fears of weak rupiah\par \par \uc2  *Bank stocks pare gains after rising on hopes of Lehman sale\par \par \uc2  By Koh Gui Qing\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 12 (Reuters) - Singapore shares <.FTSTI> rose 1.16 percent, off near two-year lows on Friday, as investors bought battered property stocks like \uc2 CapitaLand\uc2  , but a weak rupiah pushed Indonesian stocks <.JKSE> to a near 18-month low.\par \par \uc2  Singapore's Straits Times Index ended at 2,570.67 points, and Indonesian stocks dropped 3.5 percent to a level last seen in March 2007.\par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Thai stocks <.SETI> rose 1.17 percent by 0951 GMT, Philippine shares <.PSI> fell 0.99 percent, and Vietnam stocks <.VNI> dropped 4.2 percent.\par \par \uc2  In Singapore, some analysts said recent share price weakness in Southeast Asia's top developer \uc2 CapitaLand\uc2   reflected investor fears over a weakening property market in China and its impact on the firm's Chinese investments.\par \par \uc2 CapitaLand\uc2  shares have tumbled 50 percent since hitting a record level in October, and are down 32 percent for the year.\par \par \uc2  "Some residential property developers have reportedly slashed prices to generate sales," said Wilson Liew, a Kim Eng analyst. "\uc2 CapitaLand\uc2  could face short-term price pressures."\par \par \uc2  Liew has a "hold" rating for \uc2 CapitaLand\uc2 , with a price target of S$4.48, a 4.4 percent upside to Friday's closing price.\par \par \uc2 CapitaLand\uc2  ended 2.9 percent higher. Other property stocks in Singapore rose too -- City Developments  climbed 1.4 percent and Hongkong Land  gained 2.8 percent.\par \par \uc2  DBS Group , the region's largest bank, ended down 0.4 percent after rising 0.9 percent in early trade on Friday as investors bought bank stocks on hopes of a possible takeover of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  .\par \par \uc2  In Jakarta, investors dumped stocks on fears the weakening Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> signalled a worsening economic outlook as the country's current account deteriorated.\par \par \uc2  One analyst said some investors were worried \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Jusuf Kalla, \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vice-president, said on Friday the rupiah's fall was good for the country's exports.\par \par \uc2  The rupiah hit a 3-\u189\'00\'BD   week low on Tuesday at 9,370 rupiah to a dollar. It weakened 0.3 percent on Friday and was last traded at 9,450.\par \par \uc2  Heriyanto Irawan, a \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  analyst, said in a note to clients on Friday that the healthy Indonesian economy should support the rupiah in the longer term.\par \par \uc2  However, a current account deficit, weak commodity prices and fears a soft rupiah would fuel inflation might pressure the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the short run.\par \par \uc2  Telkom Indonesia , the country's largest telecoms firm, fell 4.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 PT Bank Rakyat Indonesia\uc2  Tbk  Indonesia's fourth-largest bank, was down 6.5 percent, and \uc2 PT Astra International\uc2  , the country's largest automotive distributor, dropped 10.1 percent.  (Editing by Jennifer Tan)                                                                            \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-SOUTHEASTASIA-STOCKS/|LANGEN|Table|ABN|E|RBN|SI|IF|PHD|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080912e49c001h