\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Growth worries deepen, push ringgit to 2008 low\b0\par\par\uc2 686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2008\par 04:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Aug 11 (Reuters) - Deepening worries about global growth hurt Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, particularly those of trade-dependent countries, with the Malaysian ringgit diving to its lowest in 2008 and the Singapore dollar to a five-month low.\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> fell to a one-month low while the Philippine peso <PHP=> hit a two-week low of 44.485 per dollar as the latter's broad strength overshadowed the fall in the price of oil <CLc1>, which is one of Asia's biggest imports.\par \par \uc2 The dollar struck a six-month high against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  <.DXY> as fund managers and investors unwound holdings of commodities and high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Growth concerns were pushed to the fore by last week's remarks from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  acknowledging that euro zone economies were slowing more than expected, and by suggestions the Australian authorities could be cutting rates.\par \par \uc2 Market participants seemed to be growing confident that the U.S. dollar's 6-1/2-year decline had ended, said Sean Callow, a Westpac Bank strategist.\par \par \uc2 "If this is true then the assumption that dollar/Asia must fall is being thrown into the dustbin," he said, adding that explained the profit-taking on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Westpac\uc2  does not agree that dollar has formed a cycle low but right now that's where a good amount of money is being placed," he said.\par \par \uc2 The ringgit <MYR=> hit 3.3255 per dollar, its weakest this year, and the Singapore dollar <SGD=> fell to the weaker side of 1.41 per U.S. dollar briefly after the government lowered its growth forecast for the economy.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar buying seems a bit overdone, but the dollar is still rallying against the majors," said a trader in Kuala Lumpur.\par \par \uc2 Central banks in Taiwan, \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and South Korea were suspected of having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend their falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Ravelas, chief market strategist at \uc2 Banco De Oro Universal Bank\uc2  in the Philippines, said the peso had also reacted to the dollar's broad strength.\par \par \uc2 "We are looking for a rally towards 44.50-45.00 levels in the near-term, following a near-term bottom at last week's low at 43.76," Ravelas said.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  said market positions were exacerbating the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling, owing to stop losses being triggered on long positions in the regionals.\par \par \uc2 "The low yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from open economies most leveraged to global growth are taking the brunt of the impact, while less cyclical \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  that also benefit from the oil price decline are being impacted the least," they said in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 "With positions still relatively flat and growth concerns rising, near-term momentum remain for these moves," they said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at   0749 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       109.91         109.89       -0.02\par  Sing dlr        1.4053         1.3984       -0.49\par  Taiwan dlr      31.170         31.09