\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 3-Asia cbanks prop up  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  over inflation worries\b0\par\par\uc2 954 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2008\par 09:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Central banks in South Korea, Taiwan, the Philippines and Indonesia sell dollars to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  * Central banks are concerned about the inflationary impact of rising oil prices.\par \par \uc2  * Most of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are seen remaining under pressure despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  (Updates with third \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Korea)\par \par \uc2  By Cheon Jong-woo and Melissa Chia\par \par \uc2  SEOUL/SINGAPORE, May 27 (Reuters) - Asian central banks moved to prop up falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday to help prevent surging oil prices from stoking inflation in economies bracing for a global slowdown.\par \par \uc2  Central banks in Indonesia, the Philippines, South Korea and Taiwan were spotted selling dollars to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  under pressure from inflation fears and wobbly stock markets, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said.\par \par \uc2  Asian central banks have traditionally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation which would hurt exports, the main driver of growth for most of the region's economies.\par \par \uc2  But the focus of policy in some countries is now shifting to containing inflation with oil prices at record highs above $130 a barrel driving up costs across the economy and fuelling demands for higher wages.\par \par \uc2  "In choosing between a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and potentially higher rates it would seem authorities believe higher rates would be more detrimental for growth prospects," said Magnus Prim, chief Asia strategist at SEB in Singapore.\par \par \uc2  A strong domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes imports cheaper, helping limit the inflationary impact of soaring energy costs.\par \par \uc2  "This may become a common pattern in Asia," said another analyst in Singapore. "Central banks would need to draw down their reserves, given the turn from trade surpluses to trade deficits and much more sparse capital inflows," said the analyst who declined to be named.\par \par \uc2  In South Korea, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under political pressure to cut interest rates, authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  three times in one day to support the won, Asia's second worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year after the Pakistan rupee.\par \par \uc2  Five \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers in Seoul said the Bank of Korea had sold as much as $800 million to support the won for the second time in a week. They reported two more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in the day, estimating a total of nearly $2 billion had been spent.\par \par \uc2  GROWTH DOWN, PRICES UP\par \par \uc2  The won, which has fallen 9.8 percent this year, has been under pressure from a soaring oil import bill, foreign selling of local stocks and a government plan to control overseas borrowing as the country braces for its first current account deficit since the Asian financial crisis 11 years ago.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said this month South Korea economic growth would likely slow to around 4.5 percent this year from 5.0 percent in 2007. The bank kept rates steady this month because inflation is running at a four-year high of 4.1 percent, above its target of between 2.5 percent and 3.5 percent, partly due to rising import costs.\par \par \uc2  But minutes from the Bank of Korea's meeting showed two of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's six board members wanted rates to be cut, and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  believed a weak won has more negative than positive effects on the economy.\par \par \uc2  "The won fell as oil prices jumped but we are worried that the won's fall might be excessive," Choi Jong-ku, head of the South Korean finance ministry's international bureau, told Reuters.\par \par \uc2  The won rallied on his remarks and hit a session peak after the second \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but analysts said they expected more weakness in the Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  supported by central banks on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't have a long term impact," said Han-Sia Yeo, strategist, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2  Three Manila-based traders said the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars around 43.70 per dollar, although the peso <PHP=> still fell to a low of 43.73, its weakest since November. One trader estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sold around $200 million.\par \par \uc2  The Philippine economy is feeling the heat of rising import costs and weakening demand for exports as the U.S. credit crisis and housing slump weigh on the global economy.\par \par \uc2  Imports of oil products jumped 87 percent in March while electronics exports fell almost 17 percent. The peso, Asia's top performer in 2007, has fallen 5.5 percent this year against the dollar.\par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia was also suspected of selling dollars between 9,365 and 9,380 rupiah per dollar, two traders in Jakarta said. A trader in Singapore also said \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Most central banks in the region usually do not comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  The rupiah <IDR=> has fallen 1.3 percent in the past month against the dollar despite its high yield, shunned by investors who fear that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not raise rates aggressively enough to temper inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2  The country raised its subsidised fuel prices by almost 30 percent on Saturday, making it likely that inflation which is at a 19-month high near 9 percent will soon hit double-digits.\par \par \uc2  Traders in Taiwan said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <TWD=TP>, which fell for a third consecutive session as foreigners turned net sellers of Taiwan stocks.\par \par \uc2  But portfolio outflows were the only factor weighing against a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  backed by Taiwan's huge current account surplus, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is tightening policy and an administration that promises better ties with China.\par \par \uc2  Taiwan is also abolishing price controls on petrol and diesel from June.  (Additional reporting by Kevin Yao in Singapore; Writing by Vidya Ranganathan; Editing by Tomasz Janowski)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (WRAPUP 3)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|IF|PHD|Z|ABX|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080527e45r000e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
