\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:Bank Indonesia: To Act "At Any Time" If CPI Soars   \b0\par\par\uc2 311 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2008\par 08:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds background)   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to respond to rising inflationary pressures "at any time" if necessary, its senior deputy governor told reporters Monday, suggesting that Bank Indonesia may soon turn hawkish in its policy outlook.   \par \par \uc2 "If (near-term) inflation continues to increase, Bank Indonesia has to respond to it at any time," Bank Indonesia Senior Deputy Governor Miranda Goletom said.   \par \par \uc2 She didn't state clearly whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise its key interest rate - now at 8% - to contain rising inflation.   \par \par \uc2 Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the rate unchanged for a fourth straight month after on-year inflation spiked to 8.2% in March, its highest level in 18 months.   \par \par \uc2 Her comment suggests that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't rule out future rate hikes if inflationary pressures don't abate over the next few months.   \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia so far has been hesitant to raise rates, arguing that the country's high inflation environment is mainly due to rising commodity and energy prices globally and not because of its loose monetary stance.   \par \par \uc2 It also feared that any rate hike will further damp Indonesia's economic outlook for this year, as underlined by several official forecasts made in the past week.   \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia earlier Monday predicted that economic growth will likely slow to 6.2% this year from 6.3% last year. The World Bank and the Asian Development Bank have made similar downward revisions to their 2008 gross domestic product growth forecasts for Indonesia to 6.0% from 6.4% previously.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the rupiah, hence reducing the impact of imported inflation.   \par \par \uc2 -By Farida Husna; Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com [ 07-04-08 0629GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080407e447000ad\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
