\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,280 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 06:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =UPDATE: DLR EASES AS KOREA,\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INDONESIA INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; BOJ VIGILANT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central banks in South Korea and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepped into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets Tuesday to sell dollars in favor of the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while the Bank of Japan continued its verbal support of the U.S. unit as worries over the U.S. economy and financial system continued to haunt Asian markets. (published 0411 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA PM: STRONG RUPEE HAS HURT EXPORTERS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A strong Indian rupee has hurt the revenue of exporters, but the government has taken steps to ease some pain, Prime Minister Manmohan Singh said Tuesday. (published 0441 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA WEN: CONCERNED ABOUT US DLR DEPRECIATION TREND    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Premier Wen Jiabao said Tuesday that China is concerned about the sharp decline in the U.S. dollar and is trying to gauge when it might bottom out. (published 0434 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE:JAPAN NUKAGA: CURRENT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOVES EXCESSIVE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Fukushiro Nukaga on Tuesday maintained his stepped up vigilance against the dollar's rapid fall against the yen, but still seemed steps away from taking any measures against the pair's moves. (published 0120 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S SWAN: GOVT ASSESSING RISKS OF MARKET TURBULENCE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan Tuesday said his government is assessing the risks of recent global market turbulence and it is clear that Australia's economy has been affected by the deteriorating financial circumstances. (published 0500 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: PBOC GOV: CHINA HAS ROOM TO ADJUST MONETARY POLICY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor reaffirmed Beijing's tight monetary policy Tuesday and said there was room to adjust it, but he cast doubt on market rumors about a pending revaluation of the yuan. (published 0244 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA WEN: NEED TO BALANCE ECONOMIC GROWTH, CURBING INFLATION    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Premier Wen Jiabao acknowledged Tuesday China will find it hard to achieve its targeted inflation rate of 4.8% this year, saying Beijing must strike a balance between economic growth and curbing price rises. (published 0322 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: AUSTRALIA RBA BOARD SEES NEED FOR POLICY FLEXIBILITY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reserve Bank of Australia said Tuesday that ongoing concerns about the medium-term inflation outlook prompted a decision to raise interest rates at its March 4 policy meeting. (published 0148 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: COMMONWEALTH BANK AUSTRALIA: TAX CUTS INFLATIONARY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  Chief Executive Ralph Norris said Tuesday that tax cuts have the potential to add to inflationary pressures and would be better directed into superannuation, or pensions. (published 0332 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE:PHILIPPINE JAN JOBLESS RATE UP ON QUARTER, DOWN ON YR    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The unemployment rate in the Philippines surged to 7.4% in January from 6.3% three months earlier as the number of people joining the labor force increased. (published 0214 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: NZ CANNOT ESCAPE GLOBAL SLOWDOWN, US WOES - FIN MIN    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand Finance Minister Michael Cullen said Tuesday the country cannot escape a global slowdown and the problems afflicting the U.S. economy. (published 2343 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: NZ GOVT JAN ACCTS UNDER REPORTED TAX REV BY NZ$600M    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand Finance Minister Michael Cullen said Tuesday the government's January accounts had under-reported tax revenues by NZ$600 million due to an error by the Inland Revenue Department. (published 2350 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO WITHDRAW VND52.78 TLN FROM MARKET    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The  \f28 \uc2 \f2 State Bank of Vietnam\f28 \uc2 \f2 , or the country \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said Tuesday it plans to withdraw VND52.78 trillion from financial markets soon in an effort to help control inflation. (published 0248 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM 1Q TRADE DEFICIT LIKELY TO BE RECORD-HIGH $7B -REPORT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vietnam's trade deficit is likely to widen sharply to a record-high $7 billion in the first quarter from $1.7 billion in the year-earlier period, state media reported Tuesday, citing a government official. (published 0329 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =3RD UPDATE: JAPAN GOVT PROPOSES EX-MOF TANAMI AS BOJ CHIEF    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government Tuesday newly nominated former \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official Koji Tanami as Bank of Japan governor, a selection that carries a significant risk of rejection by parliament. (published 0443 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par STRONG GROWTH KEPT SOVEREIGN DEFAULTS DOWN IN 2007 - MOODY'S    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The number of European sovereign debt issuers to default on their bonds remained at zero during 2007 despite an increase in the number of speculative grade sovereigns with outstanding bonds,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Investor's Service said in a report Tuesday. (published 0130 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA'S PARLIAMENT ADOPTS '08 NATIONAL DEVT PLAN -XINHUA    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 11th National People's Congress, China's parliament, adopted at the conclusion of its first annual session Tuesday a resolution which approves a plan of national economic and social development for 2008, China's state-run Xinhua News Agency reported. (published 0137 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN STATE GOVT PLANS A$12B METRO RAIL FOR SYDNEY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's New South Wales state government said Tuesday it plans to build a A$12 billion underground railway over the next nine years, linking the center of Sydney with its rapidly growing northwestern suburbs. (published 0206 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDONESIA TARGETS IDR2T IN ZERO-COUPON BOND AUCTION NEXT TUE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indonesian government will issue a two-year zero-coupon bond via an auction next Tuesday, the Finance Ministry said Tuesday. (published 0451 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDOSAT UPS BOND SIZE BY IDR150BLN ON GOOD DEMAND - BANKER    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PT Indonesia Satellite Corp, or  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Indosat\f28 \uc2 \f2 , has decided to increase the size of its conventional rupiah-denominated bond issue to IDR1.15 trillion from IDR1 trillion due to healthy demand, a banker said Tuesday. (published 0433 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BERLI JUCKER TO SELL THB3B 3-YR BONDS AT 3.73% THU TO TUE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Berli Jucker PCL\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a Thai manufacturer and distributor of consumer and technical products, plans to sell THB3 billion of three-year bonds to the public from Thursday to Tuesday, a company official said Tuesday. (published 0217 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA WEN:DALAI LAMA SUPPORTERS BEHIND TIBET PROTESTS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's Premier Wen Jiabao denounced supporters of the Dalai Lama Tuesday as separatists and instigators of anti-Chinese riots in Tibet's capital, after a deadline for the rioters to turn themselves in passed without any apparent surrenders. (published 0454 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ====== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ====== \par  \par =FOCUS: AUSTRALIAN BOND MARKET CALLS FOR URGENT SUPPLY BOOST    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian financial market participants have called on the government to quickly boost the supply of sovereign bonds to ease a liquidity squeeze and avert a possible future market breakdown. (published 0431 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =INTERVIEW: MA ADVISER SAYS CHINA TIES TO HELP TAIWAN ECONOMY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Presidential election front-runner Ma Ying-jeou wants to boost Taiwan's economy through better business ties with China and hopes improved relations with the mainland will give the island a bigger voice in key international groups, one of his top advisers said. (published 0359 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/18) US MULLS NEXT STEPS IN CRISIS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As jittery investors digested Washington's dramatic steps Sunday to broker a bailout of  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bear Stearns Cos.\f28 \uc2 \f2  and offer emergency credit to Wall Street firms, the possible outlines of a broader response to the U.S. financial crisis began taking shape. (published 0400 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: FED'S RATE BINGE CARRIES BIG DOLLAR RISK    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors will be mightily disappointed if the Federal Reserve cuts interest rates by anything less than 100 basis points Tuesday, but policy-makers must also consider the risk of sending the dollar into cardiac arrest. (published 0013 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =FUTURES MARKET SEES 100 BP RATE CUT TUESDAY ON CREDIT WORRIES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mounting credit stress, stemming from the demise of one of Wall Street's top five investment banks, prompted U.S. short-term interest rate futures markets Monday to fully price in expectations for a full percentage point reduction in the benchmark federal-funds lending rate on Tuesday. (published 1518 GMT) [ 18-03-08 0501GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080318e43i0007s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
