\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Oil Prices To Dent Indonesia Growth, Deficit   \b0\par\par\uc2 525 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2007\par 13:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from ministers, background.)   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Indonesia's economy will likely grow 6.2% this year, missing the government's official growth target of 6.3% due to the sharp rise in global oil prices, Bank Indonesia Governor Burhanuddin Abdullah said Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Abdullah said economic growth of 6.2% for 2007 would be "still okay" but the comments suggest higher energy costs are taking a toll on the Southeast Asian nation, a net importer of oil.   \par \par \uc2 The government maintains subsidies on domestic fuel sales to shield consumers from volatility in global energy markets. The cost of that effort will swell with higher oil prices, Finance Minister Sri Mulyani Indrawati told reporters Tuesday at a joint briefing with Abdullah.   \par \par \uc2 Indonesia next year will likely pay IDR179.4 trillion from the state budget in such subsidies, more than double the IDR85.4 trillion allocation it had previously planned, as the higher oil prices mean it will need to outlay more in subsidies, Mulyani said.   \par \par \uc2 The increased outlays won't do much damage to the government's budget. With global benchmark oil price around $100 a barrel, Indonesia's deficit would shrink to 1.3% this year from 1.5% originally forecast, as the country will receive higher profits from oil exports, Mulyani said.   \par \par \uc2 But high oil prices will likely continue weighing on rupiah against the U.S. dollar, Abdullah said.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar was at IDR9,395 at the close of Indonesian trading at 0900GMT Tuesday. By contrast, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading below IDR9,100 in mid-October.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar will likely trade around IDR9,400 until the end of the year, Abdullah predicted. But he didn't say what effect the lower rupiah might have on economic growth.   \par \par \uc2 "The devaluing of the rupiah is affected by several factors - among others, the rise in the price of oil and subprime (problems) have made investors reposition their portfolios. So this is still a movement that is normal. We know that heading toward the end of the year (investors) are always taking profit and buying (dollars) to pay debts," Abdullah said.   \par \par \uc2 He didn't comment on possible Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to defend the rupiah.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently sold dollars via state banks when the dollar reached IDR9,430, and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers say they expect the authorities to keep defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around that level.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in order to mitigate impact on the budget from the higher fuel subsidies, the government will strive to speed up its conversion of household heating fuel to liquid petroleum gas from kerosene, Mines and Energy Minister Yusgiantoro Purnomo told reporters at the same briefing.   \par \par \uc2 The government has been offering various incentives, including giving away free LPG-burning stoves to some low income households, to encourage Indonesians to switch over to the more efficient fuel for cooking and heating. That has reduced the amount of kerosene used and eased the subsidy burden on the budget while helping ease inflation.   \par \par \uc2 -By Jakarta bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; Reuben.Carder@dowjones.com [ 27-11-07 1251GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071127e3br000p8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760