\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia falls on weaker stocks, rupiah recovers   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao   \par \uc2 782 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2007\par 04:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 16 (Reuters) - Weak regional stocks weighed on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday amid worries about the health of the world's largest economy, while the Indonesian rupiah recovered after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pledged to limit its falls.   \par \par \uc2  The high-yielding Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> recovered to 9,320 per dollar from as low as 9,405, after deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Hartadi Sarwono promised to support the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  "People are selling the dollar now as they are worried that Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a trader in Jarkata.   \par \par \uc2  A second trader said more investors followed through in the later session to take profit from the dollar's recent gains.   \par \par \uc2  "I think the rupiah should settle around 9,250-9,350 near term," said the trader.   \par \par \uc2  Sarwono told Reuters that the bank would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if necessary, to stabilise the rupiah, which slid below the 9,400 level for the first time in two months and was at one point down about 1 percent from late Asian trade on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2  The rupiah, the worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia, has fallen about 4 percent versus the dollar so far this year.   \par \par \uc2  MSCI's measure of Asia Pacific stocks excluding Japan <.MIAPJ0000PUS> fell about 2.4 percent on Friday, extending its losses.   \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso <PHP=>, another high-yielder, fell as far as 43.50 per dollar, down 0.6 percent from Thursday's close.   \par \par \uc2  "Basically investors are trimming their long positions," said a trader in Manila.   \par \par \uc2  The peso remains the region's top performer with gains of almost 13 percent versus the dollar so far this year.   \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> hit 3.3895 per dollar at one point, down about 0.7 percent from late Asian trade on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Singapore dollar <SGD=> fell as far as 1.4546 per U.S. dollar, down about half of a percent.   \par \par \uc2  CORRECTION   \par \par \uc2  Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been retreating in recent sessions amid renewed jitters about the U.S. housing sector, but optimism about sustained capital inflows continued to underpin sentiment.   \par \par \uc2  "There has been a bit of risk aversion in play. But I'm pretty confident that Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will strengthen back again, this is just a correction," said Suresh Ramanathan, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at CIMB.   \par \par \uc2  "Asia is pretty much insulated from subprime, plus the fact that Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have stronger underlying demand," he said.   \par \par \uc2  The Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS> was little changed near 7.4235 per dollar, as strong investment data fuelled expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to raise interest rates again.   \par \par \uc2  Investors now look to the pace of yuan appreciation, which has quickened in recent weeks amid signs that policy makers are keen to fight inflation, to support Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  Hong Liang, China economist at  \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , said in a note that the investment bank expects the yuan to rise to 7.28 per dollar in three months, to 7.11 in six months and 6.78 in 12 months.   \par \par \uc2  "We believe that the chances are rising that policy makers will re-think the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue in the face of rising domestic inflation, renewed weakness in the dollar, mounting international pressures, and most of all, a dismal score card of trying to rebalance the economy without tackling the root cause of such imbalances in the past four years," she said.  \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0749 GMT    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       110.01         111.31       +1.18\par  Sing dlr        1.4527         1.4466       -0.42\par  Taiwan dlr      32.337         32.298       -0.12\par  Korean won      917.20         915.30       -0.21\par  Baht             33.84          33.83       -0.03\par  Peso             43.39          43.22       -0.39\par  Rupiah         9320.00        9305.00       -0.16\par  Rupee            39.33          39.31       -0.04\par  Ringgit         3.3805         3.3665       -0.41\par  Yuan            7.4235         7.4228       -0.01\par  Change so far in 2007\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       110.01         119.01       +8.18\par  Sing dlr        1.4527         1.5337       +5.58\par  Taiwan dlr      32.337         32.585       +0.77\par  Korean won      917.20         929.50       +1.34\par  Baht             33.84          36.08       +6.62\par  Peso             43.39          49.03      +13.00\par  Rupiah         9320.00        8988.00       -3.56\par  Rupee            39.33          44.26      +12.54\par  Ringgit         3.3805         3.5270       +4.33\par  Yuan            7.4235         7.8051       +5.14\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  (Editing by Jacqueline Wong