\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  c.bank says to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  if needed   \b0\par\par\uc2 372 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2007\par 07:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quotes)   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Aug 16 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if necessary to defend the rupiah <IDR=>, Bank Indonesia (BI) Governor Burhanuddin Abdullah told reporters on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 Despite the rupiah's recent fall, Abdullah said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still within its comfort range of 8,500-9,500 per dollar and that the bank would maintain its monetary policy, under which interest rates have gradually been cut in the past two years.   \par \par \uc2 Traders in Jakarta said Bank Indonesia sold dollars on Thursday to help smooth a decline in the high-yielding rupiah, which fell to 9,480 per dollar at one point, down two-thirds of a percent on the day and its weakest level since June 2006.   \par \par \uc2 It has fallen about 5.1 percent versus the dollar this year, making it the worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia, as investors cut their exposure to high-yielding but riskier assets due to worries about credit markets.   \par \par \uc2 "We will continue to smooth out volatility and we will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We will continue to be in the market," Abdullah told reporters.   \par \par \uc2 Economists said a sharp drop in the rupiah could push up inflation since prices in Indonesia were highly sensitive to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings.   \par \par \uc2 Asked whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would change its monetary stance due to the volatile markets, Abdullah said: "Inflation will still be within target, therefore there is no need to change."   \par \par \uc2 In a speech to parliament on Thursday, President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono forecast year-end inflation of 6.5 percent in 2007 against the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's forecast of 5-7 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the volatile global markets were a big factor behind the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to keep its benchmark interest rates <BIPG> unchanged this month.   \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia (BI) had cut its benchmark rate to 8.25 percent in July, the lowest since the rate was launched in July 2005, after a steady drop in inflation. It has cut the rate 13 times from a high of 12.75 percent in April last year.   \par \par \uc2 INDONESIA-ECONOMY/RUPIAH (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|IF|ABX|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070816e38g000bm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
