\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesian rupiah, peso off lows,  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  cited   \b0\par\par\uc2 269 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2007\par 04:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Feb 28 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah and Philippine peso were pushed off their lows on Wednesday by suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar selling, but both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained weak amid heightened risk aversion in global markets.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the Indonesian and Philippine central banks sold the dollar for their local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which had come under pressure as investors unwound yen-funded carry trades following a steep sell-off in global stock markets.   \par \par \uc2 By 0315 GMT, the rupiah was quoted at 9,175 per dollar <IDR=>, down 1 percent from levels seen late in Asia on Tuesday, but off a three-month low hit earlier at 9,195.   \par \par \uc2 The peso <PHP=> stood at about 48.60 to the dollar, down about 0.4 percent and just off a three-week low of 48.65. "They just want to smooth the movement, nothing more than that," said a trader in Singapore, referring to the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Philippines.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers said they suspected that Bank Indonesia had bought the rupiah around the 9,200 level against the dollar, while a trader in Manila said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  there stepped into the market when the peso weakened to 48.65 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "They were just there to dampen the volatility and prevent things from getting out of hand," the trader said.   \par \par \uc2 "The market does seem to have settled down a bit and I don't think there is any panic."   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA