\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(12/29)  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Stands Pat: Won't  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  On Rupiah  \b0\par\par\uc2 921 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 2006\par 02:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WSJA(12/29) \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Stands Pat: Won't \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  On Rupiah  \par \par \uc2 (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) By Tom Wright  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Indonesia -- The Indonesian government doesn't believe its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is overvalued and has no intention of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in financial markets as Thai authorities did to cool inflows of foreign capital into stocks, bonds and other assets, Finance Minister Sri Mulyani Indrawati said Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 'The [competitiveness] of the economy should not rely on exchange-rate policy,' she said in an interview. 'We are not manipulating the level [of the rupiah] -- we maintain policy consistency. This is what investors want to hear from us.'  \par \par \uc2 Jakarta's improved economic management has put Indonesia back on investors' radar screens, economists say. In late 2005, the government slashed costly fuel subsidies, which were weighing on state finances, and increased prices at the pump. The ensuing inflationary pressure was quickly nipped in the bud by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which recently was able to begin lowering interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The government expects inflation-adjusted economic growth of 6.3% in 2007 -- up from 5.5% in 2006 -- as lower interest rates lift consumer spending, according to Ms. Sri Mulyani. The economy relies heavily on Indonesia's huge domestic market to fuel growth, making local companies less dependent on exchange-rate policy than the region's more export-oriented corporations. Moreover, Indonesia's export growth is driven by oil, gas and other commodities that have done well this year due to strong global prices. That has pushed up the value of exports in rupiah terms, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Ms. Sri Mulyani said government spending on infrastructure projects such as roads and airports also is likely to fuel growth in the new year. In 2005, the government only spent about half of its budgeted capital expenditure; this year, the government expects to use about 85% of its capital expenditure budget, she said.  \par \par \uc2 Indonesia's economic gains and improved outlook have sparked increased international investor interest in its stock market. With plenty of liquidity in the global financial system looking for strong returns, speculative money flows have created a 'herd instinct' toward Indonesia that has driven the Jakarta Stock Exchange's benchmark index up more than 50% this year and fueled a rally in bond prices, Ms. Sri Mulyani said.  \par \par \uc2 A similar influx of short-term foreign money into stocks, bonds and commercial paper helped prompt Thailand to impose controversial capital controls on Dec. 18 in an attempt to stop a rapid appreciation in the value of the baht against the dollar. The policy triggered a sharp sell-off in the Thai stock market and forced the government to roll back provisions that made investing in the country's shares unattractive.  \par \par \uc2 Although Ms. Sri Mulyani didn't directly criticize Thailand's capital controls, she played down the effectiveness of such measures to boost export competitiveness, which was one of Thailand's goals. In particular, Thailand was concerned about declining export competitiveness with China, which has only allowed a small strengthening of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Ms. Sri Mulyani declined to blame Beijing's yuan policy for increased pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of other Asian economies. But she indicated that China should consider allowing the yuan to strengthen to avoid overheating in its own economy. 'They themselves need to adjust their own policy for their own good,' she said.  \par \par \uc2 The finance minister stressed that Indonesia wouldn't adopt measures to control short-term foreign investments. She argued that Indonesia's economic situation is different from that of Thailand. The rupiah has appreciated about 7% against the dollar this year, less than half the increase in the value of the baht. And Indonesia's propensity to suffer from bouts of inflationary pressure will also act as a natural brake on rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation in the future, she said.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, Ms. Sri Mulyani suggested Indonesian stocks are still undervalued, despite the market's recent gains, because of a perception of political risk that Indonesia has been unable to fully shake since the fall of former President Suharto in 1998. 'Indonesia has been suffering from a discount factor for quite some time,' she said.  \par \par \uc2 The minister also said the increase in the value of stocks and other financial assets wasn't translating into more direct investment in business activities, which is crucial to alleviate Indonesia's growing unemployment problem. Jakarta needs to do more to overhaul labor, tax and investment reform laws that are deterring foreign companies from investing in the manufacturing sector, she said.  \par \par \uc2 Indonesia's current economic growth rate is short of the 7% the World Bank estimates is necessary to create enough jobs for the two million Indonesians entering the work force each year. About a third of the 120 million-strong work force is either unemployed or underemployed, according to some estimates.  \par \par \uc2 'We definitely need to create more employment and that can only happen if we put more investment in manufacturing,' Ms. Sri Mulyani said.  \par \par \uc2 One key reform -- an effort to push through a new tax law that treats foreign and local businesses on an equal footing -- is bogged down in Parliament. Nevertheless, Ms. Sri Mulyani said her ministry was making progress creating special tax departments, where officials are paid higher salaries in a bid to help stamp out corruption. The ministry also is planning to crack down on Indonesians who evade taxes. 'I'm going to find any law which can punish them,' she said.  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020061229e2ct000ma\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
