\par\par \b \uc2 IMF: Lower Interest Rates Would Support Indonesian Econ  \b0\par\par\uc2 310 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2006\par 00:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--The Indonesian government has room to ease interest rates and increase public spending to support the economy, the International Monetary Fund said Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Indonesia's key challenge is raising economic growth rates to reduce unemployment and alleviate poverty, the IMF said in its latest annual review of the Indonesian economy. Though the IMF forecasts gross domestic product will pick up in the second half this year, the multilateral lender noted downside risks from any renewed financial market turmoil for developing countries and high oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF predicted Indonesia's economy will expand 5.2% for the year 2006.  \par \par \uc2 "There is scope for fiscal policy to support activity in 2006, while firmly pursuing fiscal prudence," the IMF said in a press summary of the review. The IMF urged the government to quickly implement a package of tax reforms that would make the tax system more efficient, and improve investor confidence.  \par \par \uc2 Monetary policy, too, could provide more support for the economy, as long as the timing and pace of any interest rate reductions takes into account the stability of global financial markets, the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 The fund said the value of the Indonesian rupiah seems "broadly in line with fundamentals" after some real appreciation recently. Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to build \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves was "appropriate" but the IMF said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should remain limited to smoothing volatility.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF urged \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push ahead with privatization of state-owned banks that don't have a specific public policy objective. Overall, performance by Indonesian banks has been steadily improving, the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 "In the meantime, governance could be improved by allowing private shareholders to participate in their management," the fund said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9255; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 08-07-06 1800ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020060807e2870003l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
