\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: IMF Says Indonesia Addressing Mkt Volatility  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Phelim Kyne  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2006\par 02:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Indonesia's policy-makers are taking adequate measures to protect the economy from recent shocks that reversed strong gains in the Jakarta bourse and the rupiah against the U.S. dollar, a senior International Monetary Fund official said.  \par \par \uc2 The government of President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono is implementing policies that should help cushion the economy from external turbulence, Milan Zavadjil, assistant director in the IMF's Asia and Pacific department, told Dow Jones Newswires recently.  \par \par \uc2 That confidence contributed to the IMF's decision last week to upgrade its projection for Indonesia's 2006 economic growth to 5.2% from 5.0%. The government forecasts a more robust 6.2% growth this year, outpacing last year's 5.6% expansion.  \par \par \uc2 "The impact of the last few days hasn't been (due to) Indonesia-specific factors...This sort of volatility is a concern for everybody," Zavadjil said.  \par \par \uc2 "But in principle, what we think is the government is addressing it through improvement in (Indonesia's economic) fundamentals."  \par \par \uc2 Those fundamentals include reductions in the external debt-to-GDP and public debt-to-GDP ratios, he said. The government aims to reduce its public debt-to-GDP level to around 30% by 2009 from a current level of 40%.  \par \par \uc2 Worries over rising U.S. inflation and interest rates over the past week have sent ripples through global markets, including Indonesia.  \par \par \uc2 The Jakarta Stock Exchange Composite Index fell 11% over the previous three sessions but rebounded Tuesday, ending up 1.3% at 1326.260 on bargain hunting in bank blue chips.  \par \par \uc2 The Indonesia rupiah also ended higher Tuesday as some foreign companies in Indonesia sold dollars, triggering market participants to liquidate their dollar-long positions.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar finished at IDR9,260, down from Monday's close of IDR9,300 but still above its May 17 closing low of IDR8,910.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts have blamed the Jakarta bourse's recent slide on a sudden reversal in short-term speculative inflows, or "hot money," which have flooded Indonesia since the beginning of the year due to the country's bullish economic prospects.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      IMF Says Foreign Reserves Counter Hot Money Threat \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That outlook prompted monthly hot money inflows of up to $1 billion, Coordinating Minister for the Economy Boediono said last month.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Zavadjil said Indonesia's steadily increasing foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves help mitigate the potential threat of a massive reversal in fund flows.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves rose to $42.81 billion as of April 28 from $40.08 billion as of March 29.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In fact reserves have risen by more than inflows," Zavadjil said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "So the (hot) money has come in, but (Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) can \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (to support the rupiah) when it goes out."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The IMF also supports Bank Indonesia's move earlier this month to initiate a long-awaited loosening in monetary policy by cutting its benchmark one-month rate by 25 basis points to 12.50%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts expect the Bank Indonesia benchmark rate to ease to 10.00%-11.00% by end-2006 in tandem with a projected decline in on-year inflation to around 8.00%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We support the way Bank Indonesia is bringing down interest rates in a gradual manner," Zavadjil said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They're doing the right thing to insulate themselves as much as possible against this (global economic) volatility."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That volatility hadn't shaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's resolve to continue monetary loosening, Bank Indonesia Senior Deputy Governor Miranda Gultom indicated Monday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We didn't say that we will (definitely) cut rates, but there's room (to do so)," Gultom told reporters.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Zavadjil cautioned that Bank Indonesia must balance its desire to cut rates to stimulate domestic consumption with the need to be vigilant of external macroeconomic influences that might derail economic growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A lot of things are happening (so) Bank Indonesia really has to be on its toes," he said, without elaborating.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Phelim Kyne; Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; phelim.kyne@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Mary de Wet [ 24-05-06 0017GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060524e25o0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
