\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia Mar CPI +0.03% On Mo Vs +0.31% Consensus  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,400 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2006\par 08:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Indonesia's consumer price index rose 15.74% on-year last month amid declining food prices during the seasonal harvest, the official Central Statistics Agency said Monday.  \par \par \uc2 On month, the inflation rate was 0.03%. In February, on-year inflation was 17.92% while the on-month reading was 0.58%  \par \par \uc2 Core inflation - which excludes prices that are controlled by the government, such as energy and food - was 9.64% on year.  \par \par \uc2 March inflation was below the average forecast of 16.18% by five regional analysts polled by Dow Jones Newswires. They expected on-month inflation at 0.31%. [ 03-04-06 0657GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 March's on-month inflation was the lowest such increase in two years, Central Statistics Agency Chairman Choiril Maksum told reporters at a press briefing.  \par \par \uc2 The March inflation result "was caused by the decreasing price of rice, as the harvest season has begun," Maksum said. "If we can maintain the (same) level of inflation as in March, on-year inflation by the end of the year should be good."  \par \par \uc2 Maksum didn't provide any specific forecasts for on-year inflation. He urged the government to ensure that food supply and distribution systems are functioning well to reduce potential new sources of inflation.  \par \par \uc2 The March CPI data appeared to reflect official projections that the stubbornly persistent inflationary trend will ease toward the single-digit level on year by the end of 2006. Bank Indonesia has forecast on-year inflation of around 8.0% by end-December.  \par \par \uc2 Inflation peaked at 18.38% on year in November due to the government's move to effectively raise fuel prices by more than double on Oct. 1.  \par \par \uc2 The government decided last month to shelve a politically unpopular plan to also raise electricity tariffs this year, in order to avoid a renewed surge in inflationary pressure.  \par \par \uc2 The March CPI data will likely fuel expectations that Bank Indonesia will soon start planning cuts in the one-month benchmark rate if easing inflationary pressure rolls into April.  \par \par \uc2 "Although this isn't the under the authority of (the Central Statistics Agency), I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should ease monetary tightening," Maksum said.  \par \par \uc2 However, Bank Indonesia's Board of Governors is expected to keep the benchmark lending rate unchanged at 12.75% when it holds its monthly monetary policy meeting Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts predict the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut rates beginning sometime in the second half of 2006.  \par \par \uc2 "We remain of the view that the next interest rate move by Bank Indonesia is down, not up, with the inflation data affecting the timing and amount of BI's moves, but not the direction," an ING-Barings research note issued Friday said.  \par \par \uc2 But Bank Indonesia Senior Deputy Governor Miranda Gultom said last month that the bank's tightening posture remains intact due to concerns about potential inflation-spiking "external influences," including a possible increase in the price of crude oil on the global market.  \par \par \uc2 That position has cheered analysts who've urged Bank Indonesia to maintain the tightening posture to avoid spooking offshore speculative investors who have poured into Indonesia's markets this year due to bullish sentiment about the country's economic prospects.  \par \par \uc2 Those inflows of have resulted in the dollar easing to IDR9,040, as of 0824 GMT Monday, from its end-2005 value of IDR9,835 and from a four-year high of IDR11,900 in August 2005.  \par \par \uc2 March 2006 On-Month Inflation By Sector:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Housing and energy                        +0.36% \par Food                                      -0.88% \par Processed food, beverages \par  and cigarettes                           +0.58% \par Health                                    +0.39% \par Education                                 +0.12% \par Clothing                                  +0.15% \par Transportation and Communications         +0.13% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By I Made Sentana and Farida Husna; Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Michael Kitchen [ 03-04-06 0834GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By I Made Sentana and Phelim Kyne \par    OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--The pace of Indonesia's inflation eased in March, which will likely encourage the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to maintain its current tight monetary policy with a view to cutting rates later this year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The country's consumer price index rose 15.74% from a year earlier and 0.03% from the previous month, as rice prices declined during the seasonal harvest, official Central Statistics Agency Chairman Choiril Maksum told reporters Monday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Those results compare with a consensus forecast from economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires for inflation of 16.18% on the year and 0.31% on the month.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The figures also indicate a slowing from 17.92% and 0.58% on the year and month, respectively, in February.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Core inflation, which excludes prices that are controlled by the government such as energy and food, declined to 9.64% year-on-year in March from 10.20% in February.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The issue is when they'll start cutting rates...and the most judicious thing to do would be to wait until third-quarter (2006)," said Sailesh Jha, CSFB's senior economist for Southeast Asia and India.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think it will be a progression...to about 10.25% by year-end."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia's one-month benchmark rate has stood at 12.75% since December, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tries to cool an inflationary surge which peaked in November, at 18.38% year-on-year, after the government effectively more than doubled fuel prices on Oct. 1.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The tight monetary policy has put Bank Indonesia on track to achieving its target of year-on-year inflation around 8.00% by the end of 2006. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is trying to control inflation to help the government reach its target for 6.2% economic growth in 2006.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Minister of Finance Sri Mulyani Indrawati said last week she expects the economy to expand a more conservative 5.9% in 2006, which would still outpace last year's 5.6% growth rate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Massive inflows from foreign investors bullish on Indonesia's economic prospects have also helped reduce inflation. Those inflows have resulted in the rupiah rising to IDR9,030 to the dollar at 0900 GMT Monday from its four-year low of IDR11,900/dollar in August, and IDR9,835/dollar at the end of last year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The March CPI data "...gives Bank Indonesia flexibility" in deciding when to start cutting rates, Action Economics economist David Cohen said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia's Board of Governors will review monetary policy at its monthly meeting Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Possible Inflationary Spikes Loom \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts said Bank Indonesia will hold off on any immediate cut in benchmark interest rates due to the potential for further spikes in inflation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia must allow for the possible impact of government increases in industrial fuel prices and deteriorating road infrastructure which could drive up basic food costs, Citigroup economist Anton Gunawan said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think Bank Indonesia will want to cut interest rates very soon...probably August at the earliest," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia Senior Deputy Governor Miranda Gultom said last month that the bank's tightening posture remains intact due to concerns about potential "external influences" that could drive inflation, including a possible increase in the price of crude oil on the global market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, Indonesia recorded a fairly robust trade performance in February, with the trade surplus rising to $2.84 billion from $2.4 billion a year earlier.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, the February result was below a Dow Jones Newswires consensus forecast by economists for a $3.07 billion trade surplus.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's exports eased to $7.35 billion in February from $7.51 billion in January. Imports rose to $4.51 billion from $4.27 billion in the same period.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A $376.2 million deficit in oil trade in February indicated that Indonesia, the sole Southeast Asian member of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries, is missing out on the benefits of high global crude oil prices.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, CSFB's Jha said that the Indonesia's trade performance in February was "fairly solid" and that Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't likely to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the recent strengthening in the rupiah against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think Bank Indonesia will be overly worried that the (rise) in the (rupiah) is hurting exports," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By I Made Sentana and Phelim Kyne; Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com [ 03-04-06 1127GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060403e2430008h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
